-- title:
--   Twisting a curve over a finite field by a commuting automorphism
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be a finite field, $K$ an algebraically closed field, $F_0$ a field which is a $k$-algebra, and $F$ (in a fixed universe) a field which is both a $K$-algebra and an $F_0$-algebra. Assume $F_0$ is a curve over $k$ and $F$ a curve over $K$ in the project's sense: in each case the extension has principal divisors (every nonzero element $f$ admits a divisor $D$ of degree $0$ with $D(v) = \operatorname{ord}_v(f)$ at every place $v$, a place being a proper valuation subring containing the base field whose associated local ring is a principal ideal ring), every place has residue field finite over the base field, and the module of Kähler differentials of the extension is free of rank one. Assume further that $F_0$ is generated over $k$ by a finite subset, that $K$ together with the image of $F_0$ generates $F$ (the intermediate field generated by $K$ and $\operatorname{range}(F_0 \to F)$ is all of $F$), that $\varphi \colon F \to F$ is a $K$-algebra endomorphism satisfying $\varphi(x) = x^{\#k}$ for every $x$ in the image of $F_0$, and that $\sigma$ is a $K$-algebra automorphism of $F$ with $\sigma^e = 1$ for some $e > 0$ and $\varphi \circ \sigma = \sigma \circ \varphi$. Then there exist a type $F_1$ in the same universe as $F$, a field structure on it, a $k$-algebra structure on $F_1$ and an $F_1$-algebra structure on $F$, making $F_1$ a curve over $k$ in the same sense, such that $F_1$ is generated over $k$ by a finite subset, $K$ together with the image of $F_1$ generates $F$, and $\varphi(\sigma(x)) = x^{\#k}$ for every $x$ in the image of $F_1$ in $F$. No compatibility between the structure map $F_1 \to F$ and the given $F_0$-algebra structure on $F$ is asserted.
--
--   This is the descent step producing a twisted $k$-form of the curve: for a $K$-automorphism $\sigma$ of finite order commuting with the relative $\#k$-power Frobenius $\varphi$, the composite $\varphi \circ \sigma$ is again the relative Frobenius of a finitely generated $k$-model $F_1$ of $F$, the twist of $F_0$ by the cocycle sending the Frobenius to $\sigma$. It is used in the construction of the Drinfeld curve, via [`DrinfeldCurve.exists_isCurveOver_adjoin_range_eq_top_apply_hFunctionFieldAction_eq_pow`](thm.html#DrinfeldCurve.exists_isCurveOver_adjoin_range_eq_top_apply_hFunctionFieldAction_eq_pow).
